Let then {BCDE} be the circle in the greater sphere, and {FGH} the circle in the lesser sphere. Draw two diameters in them, {BD} and {CE}, at right angles to one another. [!just I.11]

Then, given the two circles {BCDE} and {FGH} about the same center, inscribe in the greater circle {BCDE} an equilateral polygon with an even number of sides which does not touch the lesser circle {FGH}. [!just XII.16]

Let {BK}, {KL}, {LM}, and {ME} be its sides in the quadrant {BE|BCDE}. Join {KA} and carry it through to {N}. Set {AO} up from the point {A} at right angles to the plane of the circle {BCDE}, and let it meet the surface of the sphere at {O}. [!just XI.12]

Carry planes through {AO} and each of the straight lines {BD} and {KN}. They make the greatest circles on the surface of the sphere for the reason stated.

Let them make such, and in them let {BOD} and {KON} be the semicircles on {BD} and {KN}.

Now, since {OA|AO} is at right angles to the plane of the circle {BCDE}, therefore all the planes through {OA|AO} are also at right angles to the plane of the circle {BCDE}. Hence the semicircles {BOD} and {KON} are also at right angles to the plane of the circle {BCDE}. [!just XI.18]

And, since the semicircles {BED|BCDE}, {BOD}, and {KON} are equal, for they are on equal diameters {BD} and {KN}, therefore the quadrants {BE|BCDE}, {BO|BOD}, and {KO|KON} equal one another.

Therefore there are as many straight lines in the quadrants {BO|BOD} and {KO|KON} equal to the straight lines {BK}, {KL}, {LM}, and {ME} as there are sides of the polygon in the quadrant {BE|BCDE}.

Inscribe them as {BP}, {PQ}, {QR}, and {RO} and as {KS}, {ST}, {TU}, and {UO}. Join {SP}, {TQ}, {UR}, and draw perpendiculars from {P} and {S} to the plane of the circle {BCDE}. [!just IV.1; XI.11]

These will fall on {BD} and {KN}, the common sections of the planes, for the planes of {BOD} and {KON} are also at right angles to the plane of the circle {BCDE}. [!just XI.Def.4]

Let them so fall as {PV} and {SW}, and join {WV|VW}.

Now since, in the equal semicircles {BOD} and {KON}, equal straight lines {BP} and {KS} have been cut off, and the perpendiculars {PV} and {SW} have been drawn, therefore {PV} equals {SW}, and {BV} equals {KW}. [!just III.27; I.26]

But the whole {BA|AB} also equals the whole {KA}, therefore the remainder {VA} also equals the remainder {WA}. Therefore {BV} is to {VA} as {KW} is to {WA}. Therefore {WV|VW} is parallel to {KB|BK}. [!just VI.2]

And, since each of the straight lines {PV} and {SW} is at right angles to the plane of the circle {BCDE}, therefore {PV} is parallel to {SW}. [!just XI.6]

But it was also proved equal to it, therefore {WV|VW} and {SP} are equal and parallel. [!just I.33]

And, since {WV|VW} is parallel to {SP}, and {WV|VW} is parallel to {KB|BK}, therefore {SP} is also parallel to {KB|BK}. [!just XI.9]

And {BP} and {KS} join their ends, therefore the quadrilateral {KBPS} is in one plane, for if two straight lines are parallel, and points are taken at random on each of them, then the straight line joining the points is in the same plane with the parallels. For the same reason each of the quadrilaterals {SPQT} and {TQRU} is also in one plane. [!just XI.7]

But the triangle {URO} is also in one plane. If then we join straight lines from the points {P}, {S}, {Q}, {T}, {R}, and {U} to {A}, then there will be constructed a certain polyhedral solid figure between the circumferences {BO|BOD} and {KO|KON} consisting of pyramids of which the quadrilaterals {KBPS}, {SPQT}, and {TQRU} and the triangle {URO} are the bases and the point {A} is the vertex. [!just XI.2]

And, if we make the same construction in the case of each of the sides {KL}, {LM}, and {ME} as in the case of {BK}, and further, in the case of the remaining three quadrants, then there will be constructed a certain polyhedral figure inscribed in the sphere and contained by pyramids, of which the said quadrilaterals and the triangle {URO}, and the others corresponding to them, are the bases and the point {A} is the vertex.

I say that the said polyhedron does not touch the lesser sphere at the surface on which the circle {FGH} is.

Draw {AX} from the point {A} perpendicular to the plane of the quadrilateral {KBPS}, and let it meet the plane at the point {X}. Join {XB|BX} and {XK|KX}. [!just XI.11]

Then, since {AX} is at right angles to the plane of the quadrilateral {KBPS}, therefore it is also at right angles to all the straight lines which meet it and are in the plane of the quadrilateral. Therefore {AX} is at right angles to each of the straight lines {BX} and {XK|KX}. [!just XI.Def.3]

And, since {AB} equals {AK|KA}, therefore the square on {AB} equals the square on {AK|KA}. And the sum of the squares on {AX} and {XB|BX} equals the square on {AB}, for the angle at {X} is right, and the sum of the squares on {AX} and {XK|KX} equals the square on {AK|KA}. [!just I.47]

Therefore the sum of the squares on {AX} and {XB|BX} equals the sum of the squares on {AX} and {XK|KX}.

Subtract the square on {AX} from each, therefore the remainder, the square on {BX}, equals the remainder, the square on {XK|KX}. Therefore {BX} equals {XK|KX}.

Similarly we can prove that the straight lines joined from {X} to {P} and {S} are equal to each of the straight lines {BX} and {XK|KX}.

Therefore the circle with center {X} and radius on of the straight lines {XB|BX} or {XK|KX} passes through {P} and {S} also, and {KBPS} is a quadrilateral in a circle.

Now, since {KB|BK} is greater than {WV|VW}, and {WV|VW} equals {SP}, therefore {KB|BK} is greater than {SP}. But {KB|BK} equals each of the straight lines {KS} and {BP}, therefore each of the straight lines {KS} and {BP} is greater than {SP}. And, since {KBPS} is a quadrilateral in a circle, and {KB|BK}, {BP}, and {KS} are equal, and {PS|SP} less, and {BX} is the radius of the circle, therefore the square on {KB|BK} is greater than double the square on {BX}.

Draw {KZ} from {K} perpendicular to {BV}. [!just I.12]

Then, since {BD} is less than double {DZ}, and {BD} is to {DZ} as the rectangle {DB|BD} by {BZ} is to the rectangle {DZ} by {ZB|BZ}, therefore if a square is described on {BZ} and the parallelogram on {ZD|DZ} is completed, then the rectangle {DB|BD} by {BZ} is also less than double the rectangle {DZ} by {ZB|BZ}. And, if {KD} is joined, then the rectangle {DB|BD} by {BZ} equals the square on {BK}, and the rectangle {DZ} by {ZB|BZ} equals the square on {KZ}. Therefore the square on {KB|BK} is less than double the square on {KZ}. [!just I.46; III.31; VI.8.Cor]

But the square on {KB|BK} is greater than double the square on {BX}, therefore the square on {KZ} is greater than the square on {BX}. And, since {BA|AB} equals {KA}, therefore the square on {BA|AB} equals the square on {AK|KA}.

And the sum of the squares on {BX} and {XA|AX} equals the square on {BA|AB}, and the sum of the squares on {KZ} and {ZA} equals the square on {KA}, therefore the sum of the squares on {BX} and {XA|AX} equals the sum of the squares on {KZ} and {ZA}, and of these the square on {KZ} is greater than the square on {BX}, therefore the remainder, the square on {ZA}, is less than the square on {XA|AX}. [!just I.47]

Therefore {AX} is greater than {AZ|ZA}. Therefore {AX} is much greater than {AG}.

And {AX} is the perpendicular on one base of the polyhedron, and {AG} on the surface of the lesser sphere, hence the polyhedron does not touch the lesser sphere on its surface.

Therefore, given two spheres about the same center, a polyhedral solid has been inscribed in the greater sphere which does not touch the lesser sphere at its surface.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
